# 20. Final acceptance criteria

GitQuest Lab is complete when:

1. A beginner can learn Git fundamentals through guided visual lessons.
2. The main Playground uses a full-screen, responsive layout.
3. The left navigation is small, collapsible, and secondary to the workspace.
4. The working directory, staging area, local repository, and remote repository are visible together on desktop.
5. The commit graph and terminal are visible together without requiring global page scrolling.
6. Supported commands update the simulator's repository state accurately.
7. Local and remote histories are independent until an operation connects them.
8. Learners can practise push, fetch, pull, divergence, rejected pushes, and collaboration scenarios.
9. Git animations show meaningful cause and effect.
10. All topics in both supplied curriculum guides are traceable to the learning system.
11. All 80 interview questions remain accessible.
12. All required production troubleshooting scenarios are represented.
13. Users can practise corresponding commands on their own Windows, macOS, or Linux machines.
14. Browser simulation and real-machine execution are clearly distinguished.
15. Progress tracking and scenario reset work reliably.
16. The application passes relevant tests and builds for Vercel deployment.
17. No essential feature is represented by a nonfunctional button or fabricated success message.

**Begin by inspecting the current repository, reviewing the curriculum files, and creating the implementation plan and scenario coverage matrix. Then implement the full-screen workspace first, followed by the Git simulator and remote repository behavior. Prioritize a compact, coherent, technically accurate learning experience over adding decorative UI or superficial feature counts.**